# `cp .env.example .env` gives `docker compose` its interpolation values. Nothing on the
# `make deps-up` path reads the copy — not the Makefile, not scripts/db_init.py, not any
# Python at import — so `make deps-up` straight after the copy fails with `FATAL:
# PROXYSHOP_WORKER is unset`. Load it into the shell first — this is step 1 of
# docs/deploy.md and of docs/demo/starting-slice.md, and it is one line:
#
#     set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# ONE OTHER THING READS `.env`, and it is worth knowing before you edit the port block:
# scripts/verify.sh sources it with `set -a` on every `make verify`, exporting every
# assignment here into the test process and shielding only PROXYSHOP_WORKER. A `.env`
# carrying relocated ports therefore re-points the whole suite at a stack that may not be
# running — measured at 133 skips and 2 failures against 0 skips with no `.env` present.
# An earlier revision of this header claimed "no script sources it"; that was wrong, and it
# was wrong because it was reasoned from a grep for `dotenv` across *.py when the loader is
# shell. docs/deploy.md carries the measurement.
#
# Worker index (D38). Every worker owns its own Postgres database (proxyshop_w<N>), its own
# Redis logical DB and its own `w<N>:` key prefix, so two things never share state.
#
# NOT 0. Worker 0 is RESERVED for this project's scored measurement run; a demo or a
# deployment that takes it corrupts the number the project is graded on. 1 is the default
# here and is what both runbooks now tell an operator to use. (An earlier revision of
# docs/demo/starting-slice.md said `export PROXYSHOP_WORKER=0` and disagreed with this line;
# the runbook was the side that was wrong.)
PROXYSHOP_WORKER=1

# The four least-privilege roles' password has ONE source of truth (T-112). It is seeded by
# db/init/00-roles.sql on a fresh volume and by db/migrations/0001 in every worker database,
# forwarded into the postgres container by docker-compose.yml, and read back on the connect
# side by proxyshop_support.postgres.role_dsn — all from this one variable. Leave it UNSET to
# get the documented dev default, whose single copy lives in 00-roles.sql's coalesce();
# setting it here (or in the shell before `make deps-up`) changes the seed and the connect
# side together. It must be set BEFORE the pgdata volume is created: the initdb hook runs
# once.
#
# LEAVING IT UNSET NOW ACTUALLY WORKS IN THE CONTAINERS TOO, which it did not before. The
# service fragments used to interpolate `${PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD:-}` into their DSNs, so an
# unset variable produced `postgresql://exchange:@postgres:5432/...` — an EXPLICITLY EMPTY
# password, which libpq rejects before it reaches the server (`fe_sendauth: no password
# supplied`). docs/deploy.md therefore told operators the variable was REQUIRED while this
# file told them to leave it unset, and `cp .env.example .env` produced the configuration
# that could not serve. The fragments now carry password-free DSNs and resolve the credential
# at container start through role_dsn, the same function the T-112 tests pin, so the two
# files agree and the default path is the working one.
# PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD=

# Postgres (D38). The DATABASE component below is ADVISORY: proxyshop_support.postgres
# rewrites it to proxyshop_w$PROXYSHOP_WORKER on every connection, so a .env copied between
# worktrees can never put two workers in one database. Host and port are taken from these
# values; all five are optional (the compose defaults are identical). NONE of the five
# carries a password on purpose (T-112) — a literal in a file every worktree copies is a
# copy that stops agreeing with the cluster the moment the credential changes. The four
# role passwords come from PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD above; admin's comes from
# proxyshop_support.postgres.ROLES, which a test pins to docker-compose.yml's
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Add a password back here only to override one role, and know that
# PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD outranks it.
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_ADMIN=postgresql://proxyshop@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_EXCHANGE=postgresql://exchange@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_TRUST_RW=postgresql://trust_rw@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_VAULT=postgresql://buyer_vault@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_APP=postgresql://app@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
NEO4J_URI=bolt://localhost:7687
NEO4J_USER=neo4j
NEO4J_PASSWORD=proxyshop_dev_pw
# No database component on purpose (D39): the logical DB index is the worker index and
# proxyshop_support.redis_client sets it authoritatively. A `/1` here used to be
# silently authoritative instead, landing every worker on DB 1 to FLUSHDB each other.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
SHOPIFY_STUB_URL=http://localhost:8787
# D19 as amended by D56: the default is `lexical` (offline, dependency-free, and it RANKS —
# `hash` inverts 28 of 45 relevant/irrelevant pairs in the ranking gate). Left blank on purpose
# rather than spelled out: `get_embedding_provider` treats unset and blank alike and falls
# through to DEFAULT_PROVIDER, so this file documents the knob without pinning a second copy of
# the default that a swap would then silently miss. Set it to `hash` for byte-identity work, or
# to `local_bge` for the real model (optional `embeddings` extra; `make e2e-live` sets it).
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=
LLM_PROVIDER=double
BUYER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
STORE_AGENT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# The hosted store agent advocates for exactly ONE store and the `BidRequest` on the wire does
# not name one, so the store context is deployment configuration. Both are empty by default and
# an empty STORE_AGENT_CONTEXT is a decision, not an error: the agent answers every solicitation
# `204 store_context_unconfigured` and `collect_bids` represents it at list price (R10). Point
# the first at a JSON store context INSIDE the container (see packages/store-agent/compose.yaml
# for the volume that puts it there); set the second when that file states no domain of its own
# — without a domain the agent emits no `checkout_url` and the exchange's C10 check drops the
# bid from every shortlist.
STORE_AGENT_CONTEXT=
STORE_AGENT_STORE_DOMAIN=
INTERVIEW_MODEL=claude-opus-5
EXTRACT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# The live Anthropic client's three settings (packages/llm/src/config.py:84-88), read by
# nothing until LLM_PROVIDER above is changed from its `double` default.
#
# AN UNSET KEY IS NOT A BROKEN STACK, and that is the point of writing it down empty rather
# than leaving it out: D19/D20 make the offline deterministic double the default provider, so
# `build_llm(...)` returns a `DeterministicLLM` that opens no socket, and the whole suite runs
# that way (D3 forbids a verify that reaches the network). The key is read ONLY under
# `LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic`, and only on the first `complete()` call — where an empty one is a
# legible MissingApiKeyError naming this variable, never a silent fallback to something live.
#
# IT IS NOW FORWARDED BY COMPOSE, and this paragraph used to say the opposite. It said "no
# service image installs the `anthropic` SDK, so `LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic` inside a container
# raises at packages/llm/src/client.py:168 whatever the key says. The live path is a local
# `.venv` run." That was true when it was written and is now false for the two images that
# carry a voice: the buyer service and the store agent both ship the SDK, both import `llm` at
# module scope, and `apps/buyer/compose.yaml` and `packages/store-agent/compose.yaml` forward
# LLM_PROVIDER and this key. `LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic` in a container is a live path now.
#
# This file is TRACKED — leave the value empty here and put a real key in `.env`, which is
# not. A key committed to this file is a key published to everyone with a clone.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Blank means the documented default: 1024 tokens and 60.0 seconds. A non-numeric or
# non-positive value raises at load rather than being rounded into something plausible.
LLM_MAX_TOKENS=
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

CHECKOUT_MODE=redirect

# The exchange's composition root (apps/exchange/src/composition.py). Point ONE of these at a
# deployment document and `uvicorn exchange.main:app` binds the collaborators it names — the
# seller registry (eligibility + registered checkout domains + where each store agent answers),
# the trust service's blacklist projection, and the checkout mode. Left unset — which is the
# default and is what a fresh checkout gets — the exchange keeps every fail-closed default:
# every store is denied `unavailable`, nothing is ranked and nothing can be accepted. That is
# the correct posture for an exchange nobody has configured, and it is why these are commented
# out rather than given a value.
#
#   {"sellers": [{"store_id": "s1", "eligibility": "eligible",
#                 "registered_domain": "s1.example.com",
#                 "bid_endpoint": "http://store-agent-s1:8080/v1/bid-requests"}],
#    "trust_snapshot": {"s1": {"store_id": "s1", "blacklisted": false, "score": 0.8}},
#    "checkout_mode": "redirect"}
#
# EXCHANGE_AUCTION_STORE selects where auction records live: `redis` for the durable store
# (D39's per-worker Redis), anything else for the in-process one. Compose sets `redis`.
#
# The in-process store is BOUNDED — 512 records, the auction's own 15-minute TTL, oldest-first
# eviction — so it cannot grow without limit the way it once could on an unauthenticated
# `POST /auctions`. What it still is, is PROCESS-LOCAL: restart the exchange and every open
# auction and every one-accept reservation goes with it. An unreachable Redis is a 503 naming
# the store, never a quiet downgrade back to memory.
EXCHANGE_AUCTION_STORE=redis

# EXCHANGE_DEPLOYMENT is a path (inside the container, when running under compose);
# EXCHANGE_DEPLOYMENT_JSON is the same document inline and needs no mount. The path wins if
# both are set. A named-but-unreadable path, or a document with an unrecognised eligibility
# word, a registered domain carrying a scheme, a non-boolean `blacklisted` or an unregistered
# checkout mode, is a 503 naming the row — never a silently empty shortlist.
# EXCHANGE_DEPLOYMENT=
# EXCHANGE_DEPLOYMENT_JSON=
SEED_CATEGORY=coffee

# The five published rank weights and their version string. These REPLACE a single
# `RANKING_WEIGHTS=w_m=0.35,w_e=0.20,w_t=0.20,w_v=0.15,w_d=0.10` line that nothing read: the
# published loader is `contracts.ranking.RankingWeights.from_env`, and `WEIGHT_ENV_VARS`
# (packages/contracts/src/ranking.py:58-64) names these five SEPARATE variables plus
# `RANK_WEIGHTS_VERSION`. A weight set written into the old name changed no shortlist
# anywhere; `apps/exchange/compose.yaml` already forwards the five real ones.
#
# Left EMPTY rather than carrying the published numbers, for the reason PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD
# above is: `from_env` skips a blank variable and falls back to the published set, so empty
# here means "whatever packages/contracts publishes", while a copy of the numbers is a second
# source of truth that stops agreeing the day the published set moves. A malformed value, or a
# set that does not sum to 1.0, raises at load rather than defaulting silently.
RANK_W_M=
RANK_W_E=
RANK_W_T=
RANK_W_V=
RANK_W_D=
RANK_WEIGHTS_VERSION=

# The bearer token the merchant service's administrative routes require — discount-code
# minting (`POST /codes`), the envelope routes and the kill switch. It appeared in NO compose
# fragment and NO .env.example at all, so those routes were unreachable in every deployment:
# apps/merchant/svc/src/install/routes.py:63 reads it with no default and no fallback, on
# purpose, so an unset value makes the route refuse every caller with
# `503 {"error":"admin-api-not-configured"}` rather than opening itself.
#
# This is a DEV value and it is not a secret. It is spelled here — unlike the Postgres role
# passwords, which are deliberately absent from this file — because there is nowhere else for
# it to come from: there is no seeded default anywhere in the tree, so a blank here just
# reproduces the 503. Replace it anywhere the merchant API is reachable from outside a
# laptop.
MERCHANT_ADMIN_TOKEN=dev-merchant-admin-token

# Published host ports (D9: every one verified free on this host at scaffold time;
# D40: all env-overridable, and no test may hard-code a port).
PG_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379
NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474
NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687

# The service ports. These were documented only in docs/deploy.md ("The new ports are not in
# .env.example — that file belongs to another lane"), which meant the one file an operator
# copies did not mention the ports the stack publishes. Moving the whole block is how a
# second checkout runs its own stack alongside this one (D40).
BUYER_SVC_PORT=8081
MERCHANT_SVC_PORT=8082
EXCHANGE_PORT=8083
TRUST_PORT=8084
INGEST_PORT=8085
STORE_AGENT_PORT=8086
SHOPIFY_STUB_PORT=8787

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cross-service wiring that three features need and no shipped artifact carried until now.
# Same shape as MERCHANT_ADMIN_TOKEN above: the code was built, tested and served, and the
# one file an operator copies never mentioned the key, so the feature was dead on arrival in
# every deployment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the MERCHANT publishes verified order webhooks as trust ledger events. The merchant's
# composition root falls back to this same value, so the shipped stack works with it unset;
# it is named so a deployment whose trust service is not the compose service can move it.
# An unreachable endpoint is counted and logged, never raised — a raising sink is answered
# 500 and Shopify retries the delivery for 48 hours.
TRUST_URL=http://trust:8084

# R13's address book: where each store's agent receives its OWN trust deltas, as a JSON
# object {store_id: base_url}. No wildcard, ever — the only alternative to an address is a
# guess, and a guessed address is how one store's trust movement reaches a competitor. A
# store absent from this map is simply not told. Trust never broadcasts.
#
# LEAVING THIS BLANK NO LONGER SWITCHES THE LOOP OFF. `apps/trust/compose.yaml` now carries a
# default naming the four `demo`-profile store agents, and compose's `${VAR:-default}` treats
# blank exactly as unset — so `cp .env.example .env && docker compose --profile demo up` runs
# a market whose shops actually learn. Blank here means "use the stack's own address book".
#
# It was blank everywhere before, and that was the defect rather than a default: this key had
# five occurrences in the repository and not one of them set a value, so the store agents' only
# learning input was dead in every shipped configuration — and nothing logged, counted, or
# health-reported that it was off. D55 makes a dedicated advocate the service a shop BUYS by
# joining the network; an advocate that is never told what its store's score did is that claim
# unwired. The trust service now says which of the two halves of its learning loop are
# addressed in a log line at boot, and serves the whole delivery condition — including the
# ring of deltas that did NOT land, with the reason for each — on `GET /events/verify` under
# `store_agent_notifications`.
#
# To run agents this stack does not define, or to genuinely turn the push off, set it here:
#   TRUST_STORE_AGENT_ENDPOINTS={"s1":"http://store-agent-s1:8080"}   # one agent
#   TRUST_STORE_AGENT_ENDPOINTS={}                                    # off, and said so at boot
TRUST_STORE_AGENT_ENDPOINTS=

# The OTHER half of the learning loop: where a completed purchase's trust movement becomes a
# bandit outcome. The exchange serves and publishes `POST /internal/outcomes` — "trust reports
# an outcome back to the exchange for the bandit update" — and it had no production producer
# anywhere in the repository. Its only callers were tests and the e2e harness, so
# `exchange.policy.bandit.update` never ran in a deployment: every posterior sat on its seeded
# prior for the life of the process and exposure never shifted with results.
#
# The exchange's base url is enough; the path is appended by the trust service. Blank falls
# through to `apps/trust/compose.yaml`'s default (http://exchange:8083). Set it to nothing at
# all — `TRUST_EXCHANGE_OUTCOMES_URL` removed from the environment — only in a deployment with
# no exchange, and expect the boot line that says no purchase will ever move a posterior.
TRUST_EXCHANGE_OUTCOMES_URL=

# R5 magic-link sign-in for the buyer UI. With SMTP_URL and SENDER unset the auth routes
# answer a deliberate 503 and the journey page shows the service's own words rather than
# failing blankly — so an operator who has not configured mail gets a legible page, not a
# broken one.
#
# LEAVE THESE BLANK HERE and export the real values instead. Two reasons, and the second is
# not obvious: a secret does not belong in a tracked file, and `apps/buyer/devstack/run.py`
# only self-configures its console transport when BOTH _SMTP_URL and _TRANSPORT are blank in
# the environment — `scripts/verify.sh` sources this file with `set -a`, so a value written
# here silently turns the local devstack demo into a 503.
#
# SMTP_URL is the MTA as a URL. Two schemes, and both are TLS:
#
#   smtp://host:587    submission; this service issues STARTTLS and REQUIRES the upgrade.
#                      An MTA that will not offer it ends the delivery rather than being
#                      sent a sign-in token in the clear.
#   smtps://host:465   implicit TLS, encrypted from the first byte.
#
# The certificate is VERIFIED in both cases — chain and hostname. It was not before: smtplib
# builds an unverified context when it is handed none, so anything able to answer for the
# MTA's address could take the SMTP password and read every sign-in token.
#
# A credential may ride in the URL (`smtp://user:pass@host:587`, percent-encoded) but the
# two variables below are better and, for most providers, necessary: SES-style passwords
# contain `/` and `+`, and a raw `/` in a URL's userinfo re-parses the whole URL into a
# different host. Set the credential in exactly ONE place; stating it twice is a boot failure
# rather than a silent preference, so a rotation cannot leave the stale copy in use.
#
# SENDER is the envelope sender and the `From:` header; a display name is fine
# (`Proxyshop <no-reply@yourdomain>`). It must be an identity the provider has verified for
# your account, or the first `DATA` gets a 550 and no buyer ever receives a link. For the
# mail to survive a spam filter the sending domain also needs SPF and DKIM published — the
# provider's dashboard gives you both records; this service cannot supply them for you.
#
# Worked settings for two common providers, so the shape is unambiguous. NEITHER IS
# CONFIGURED HERE and neither account exists — copy the shape, export your own values:
#
#   # SendGrid — the username is the literal word `apikey` for every account
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_TRANSPORT=smtp
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_URL=smtp://smtp.sendgrid.net:587
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_USERNAME=apikey
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_PASSWORD='SG.xxxxxxxx'      # the API key
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SENDER='Proxyshop <no-reply@yourdomain>'
#
#   # Amazon SES — the SMTP credential is NOT your AWS access key; generate it in the SES
#   # console. The region in the host has to match the region the identity is verified in.
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_TRANSPORT=smtp
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_URL=smtp://email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:587
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_USERNAME='<your-ses-smtp-username>'
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_PASSWORD='<base64+with/slashes=>'   # quote it
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SENDER=no-reply@yourdomain
#
# A new SES account is in the sandbox and can only send to addresses IT has verified, and an
# unverified SENDER is refused the same way on any provider. Both surface as `502` from
# `POST /buyer/auth/magic-link` with the MTA's own `550` in the service log — the response
# deliberately does not repeat it, because "which address was refused" on an unauthenticated
# route is an oracle. `docker compose logs buyer-svc | grep 'delivery failed'` is where the
# reason is, and it is the first thing to read when links stop arriving.
#
# To try the whole path with no provider at all, run any local SMTP sink (`python -m
# aiosmtpd -n -l 127.0.0.1:8025`, mailpit, mailhog) and point at it with STARTTLS off:
#
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_TRANSPORT=smtp
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_URL=smtp://127.0.0.1:8025
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_STARTTLS=disabled
#   export PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SENDER=no-reply@proxyshop.example
#
# That exact path — request a link, an email really delivered, redeem it, get a session — is
# what `apps/buyer/svc/tests/test_auth_magic_link_smtp.py` drives against a real socket, so
# it is checked on every run rather than only when somebody tries it.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_URL=
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SENDER=

# The SMTP credential, kept out of the URL where it cannot be spelled. Both or neither: half
# a credential is a boot failure, because an MTA asked to authenticate with only one of the
# two answers 535, which looks exactly like a wrong password.
#
# The PASSWORD is used BYTE FOR BYTE — it is the one variable this service does not strip,
# because quietly altering a credential is how a service ends up authenticating as something
# nobody configured. A trailing newline from `$(cat secret)` is therefore a real character
# and you get a 535; the delivery-failure log line says so.
#
# NEVER fill these in here. This file is tracked; export them, or mount them as a secret.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_USERNAME=
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_PASSWORD=

# Whether a plain `smtp://` session must be upgraded to TLS. Accepts exactly `required` or
# `disabled`; blank is `required`. Note which way that falls, because it is the opposite of
# TRANSPORT below: saying nothing about TLS gets you TLS, and turning it OFF is what has to
# be named. A near miss — `off`, `false`, `0`, `Disabled` — is a boot failure, because every
# plausible reading of it is a request to stop encrypting a session that carries live sign-in
# tokens.
#
# `disabled` is for a sink on the same machine, where the session never touches a network.
# Pointed anywhere else it publishes every sign-in link to whatever is on the path, and the
# service says so at boot, in a block on stdout. With a credential configured it is not
# allowed at all against a remote host: a provider password is the standing right to send
# mail as this deployment, which is not comparable to one buyer's fifteen-minute link.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_STARTTLS=

# Extra certificate authorities to trust, as a path to a PEM file — for an MTA whose
# certificate no public CA issued, such as a company's internal submission host. ADDED to the
# system trust store rather than replacing it, so setting it never silently stops a public
# provider from being verified. A path that cannot be read is a boot failure and not a quiet
# fallback to trusting less. Leave blank for every hosted provider.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_SMTP_CA_BUNDLE=

# WHICH transport, stated. Accepts exactly `smtp` or `console` and NOTHING else — a blank is
# "I have not decided" (read as the SMTP variables above, which fail closed), and any other
# value, including a near miss like `Console` or `consol`, stops the process rather than
# being interpreted.
#
#   smtp     mail the link through SMTP_URL. Same as leaving this blank, except that a
#            deployment which says `smtp` and configures no MTA is a boot failure instead of
#            a service that answers 503 to every login while looking configured.
#   console  PRINT the sign-in link, token and all, to the service's stdout.
#
# WHAT `console` COSTS, stated plainly because it is easy to leave on. It prints the whole
# sign-in link — token included — to this process's stdout. The token is a live single-use
# bearer credential, so ANYONE WHO CAN READ THE SERVICE LOG CAN SIGN IN AS ANY ADDRESS THEY
# TYPE. Not "as themselves": they request a link for someone else's address, read the token
# out of the log, and redeem it. There is no password to fail and no second factor. `docker
# compose logs buyer-svc`, a log shipper, a CI artifact, a container journal, a screen share,
# and anyone with shell on the host are all complete authentication bypasses while it is set.
#
# That is acceptable on a laptop, where stdout is one terminal belonging to the person who is
# also the buyer. It is not acceptable on any host reachable by anybody else, and a hosted
# demo is exactly such a host. A public deployment running `console` has no authentication at
# all, however finished the login page looks. The service shouts a banner at boot when it is
# set, so `docker compose logs buyer-svc | head` tells you which of the two you are running.
#
# Retiring it is this section: set TRANSPORT=smtp and the four SMTP variables above. Nothing
# else about the deployment changes — the same routes, the same links, the same single-use
# fifteen-minute redemption — only where the link goes.
#
# apps/buyer/devstack/run.py sets this to `console` for you (and points the base URL below at
# its own origin), because a workstation has no MTA and the journey's confirm step is gated
# on a session. Setting either variable yourself leaves the launcher's hands off both — which
# is also why this must stay BLANK in this file: `scripts/verify.sh` sources it with `set -a`
# and a value written here reaches the launcher's environment.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_TRANSPORT=

# MUST be the buyer UI's ORIGIN ROOT, not a sub-path. The page reads its token off its own
# `?token=` query string, there is no server-side callback route, and the SPA is mounted at
# `/` with no fallback — so a sub-path base URL 404s before the page ever loads and every
# link handed out is dead. Keep the trailing slash and keep it aligned with the port that
# actually serves the SPA.
#
# The port to match is whichever one SERVES THE SPA, which is not the API's. Under
# apps/buyer/compose.yaml that is the `buyer-web` container on BUYER_WEB_PORT (default 8080),
# NOT the buyer service on BUYER_SVC_PORT — this comment used to name the service and its port,
# from before a separate web container existed. Compose builds its own default for this variable
# from BUYER_WEB_PORT (`apps/buyer/compose.yaml:123`), so setting it here OVERRIDES that, and a
# stale value here is worse than an empty one.
#
# 8080 is `buyer-web`, the nginx container that serves the built SPA and reverse-proxies
# `/buyer/` to buyer-svc. That is where the PAGE lives, and a magic link is a page the shopper
# opens — pointed at 8081 it reaches the API, which serves no page, and the shopper gets a 404
# holding a credential that is now spent. It was 8081 until the demo stack landed.
#
# 8080 is equally WRONG for the devstack, which serves page and API together on 8100 — so
# apps/buyer/devstack/run.py overrides this with `http://127.0.0.1:8100/` rather than
# inheriting a link that would 404. Two deployments, two origins, and neither inherits the
# other's: whatever serves the PAGE is what belongs here.
PROXYSHOP_BUYER_MAGIC_LINK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/

# The buyer service's composition root (apps/buyer/svc/src/composition.py:254-277). EXCHANGE_URL
# — which apps/buyer/compose.yaml already sets — is the LOWEST of three rungs: it says where the
# exchange is and NOTHING about who competes, so a buyer configured by it alone opens every
# auction over an EMPTY candidate set. These four carry the rest, and no shipped artifact
# carried any of them until now.
#
# BUYER_DEPLOYMENT is a path to the document (a path INSIDE the container under compose, so a
# file one has to be mounted); BUYER_DEPLOYMENT_JSON is the same document inline and the path
# outranks it. Keys: exchange_url, roster, request_timeout_seconds. BUYER_ROSTER /
# BUYER_ROSTER_JSON carry the roster ALONE and are read only when the document states none —
# a roster is data, not settings. Each row needs a `store_id` and a `list_price` above zero
# (`max_discount_pct` optional), because that is how the exchange will read it:
#
#   BUYER_DEPLOYMENT_JSON='{"exchange_url":"http://exchange:8083","request_timeout_seconds":20}'
#   BUYER_ROSTER_JSON='[{"store_id":"s1","list_price":19.99}]'
#
# Empty is exactly unset here — every reader is `str(environ.get(...) or "").strip()` — and
# unset is the deliberate fail-closed posture: every confirmation is the 503 it always was
# rather than a guessed candidate set. A NAMED-but-unreadable path is refused loudly instead,
# because that is a misconfiguration rather than a deployment that stated nothing.
BUYER_DEPLOYMENT=
BUYER_DEPLOYMENT_JSON=
BUYER_ROSTER=
BUYER_ROSTER_JSON=

# The built buyer UI's directory, read by `composition.mount_ui` (composition.py:278) and by
# nothing else. Deliberately in NO compose fragment: the shipped container runs
# `uvicorn buyer_svc.main:app`, which never calls `mount_ui`, so setting it there would serve
# nothing. The only caller is the devstack launcher `apps/buyer/devstack/run.py`, which sets
# this itself from the vite build and unsets it when there is none. Blank, missing or
# nonexistent mounts nothing and is NOT an error — the API is the service and the UI is a
# build artefact that may not have been built yet.
BUYER_UI_DIST=

# The ingest scheduler's three variables (services/ingest/src/scheduler/{catalog,cadence,
# state}.py). `ingest.main` globs `*/routes.py`, so `scheduler/routes.py` is mounted in the
# shipped image and reads all three AT IMPORT; none appeared in any compose fragment or here,
# so the crawler shipped with nothing to crawl. All three treat blank as unset:
#
#   PROXYSHOP_INGEST_STORES  a JSON array of store targets (store_id, base_url, source:
#     signed_fetch|catalog_mcp, ...). UNSET = an EMPTY registry: `POST /refresh/{store_id}`
#     answers 404 for every store and a tick refreshes nothing. A malformed entry is skipped
#     and logged rather than raised, so one typo cannot take the service down at import.
#   PROXYSHOP_INGEST_CADENCE  inline JSON (it starts with `[`) or a path to a file holding it.
#     UNSET = the published DEFAULT_CADENCE: price/availability hourly, product fields daily,
#     policy claims weekly. That is a working table, not a stall.
#   PROXYSHOP_INGEST_SCHEDULER_STATE  a path for the durable schedule. UNSET = an in-memory
#     store that RESETS ON RESTART, so the first tick after a redeploy re-crawls every
#     configured store. The service says exactly that in a startup warning.
#
# Example: PROXYSHOP_INGEST_STORES='[{"store_id":"s1","base_url":"https://s1.example.com"}]'
PROXYSHOP_INGEST_STORES=
PROXYSHOP_INGEST_CADENCE=
PROXYSHOP_INGEST_SCHEDULER_STATE=
